From 7c3921175b2a15000446eda760eddefe39f74882 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 06:08:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: the last payload byte of each InfoFrame that the EVO1
 HAL sends

Evo1SendHdmiInfoFrame() builds the packet for the HDMI library as the
header (HB0..HB2), the checksum it inserts (PB0) and the payload
(PB1..PBn), in a buffer of infoframeSize + 1 bytes, where infoframeSize is
the size of the header and the payload. It sums the checksum over those
infoframeSize + 1 bytes, but it handed the library infoframeSize of them,
and the library copies exactly the length it is given into the zeroed
packet that it writes to the registers of the SOR (hdmiPacketWrite9171()):
the last payload byte reached the registers as 0.

This is the HAL that sends the InfoFrames of Maxwell, Pascal and Volta
with this driver (nvEvo94, nvEvo97, nvEvoC3), and the one through which
Hades sends its vendor InfoFrames in the generic slot
(nvHadesEvo1SendHdmiGenericInfoFrame()). What was lost:
- PB26 of the HDR InfoFrame, the high byte of MaxFALL: a MaxFALL of
  256 cd/m2 or more went out modulo 256 (400 as 144);
- PB27 of the HDR10+ vendor InfoFrame that Hades sends in the generic slot
  (HadesSendHdr10Plus()): its graphics overlay and no-delay flags;
- the last byte of the vendor InfoFrame that a client gives
  (NV_HDMI_VSIF_METADATA), in the vendor-specific slot or in the generic
  one: the 3D_Structure of a top-and-bottom HDMI vendor InfoFrame of five
  bytes, which then said frame packing;
- PB13 of the AVI InfoFrame (the high byte of the start of the right bar),
  0 in what NVKMS builds;
- the last byte of NVKMS's own vendor InfoFrames, a reserved byte that
  NvTiming_ConstructVendorSpecificInfoframe() appends as 0.
The checksum on the wire is the hardware's (NVKMS asks for it, CHKSUM_HW,
whenever it asks for a checksum), so the packet that went out was
consistent; its last byte was the one that was wrong.

The largest InfoFrame here, a vendor InfoFrame with 27 bytes of payload, is
31 bytes with its checksum, what the library takes
(NVHDMIPKT_CTAIF_MAX_PKT_BYTES); the AVI InfoFrame of 13 bytes is 17, what
the AVI writer of the 9171 class takes. The EMP of HDMI VRR has no checksum
and was given whole. Measured on the host with NVIDIA's packet library
writing to a register file: the registers change only where the byte that
was lost was not 0.
---
 src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-evo1.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-evo1.c b/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-evo1.c
index 233f2c6..de75131 100644
--- a/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-evo1.c
+++ b/src/nvidia-modeset/src/nvkms-evo1.c
@@ -715,13 +715,21 @@ static void Evo1SendHdmiInfoFrame(const NVDispEvoRec *pDispEvo,
         infoframe[headerSize] = ~checksum + 1;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Hades: the library is given the whole packet, the checksum inserted
+     * above included: infoframeSize is the size of the header and the
+     * payload, and the library copies no more than the length it is given
+     * into the packet it writes to the registers (hdmiPacketWrite9171()).
+     * Given infoframeSize alone, the last payload byte went out as 0.
+     */
     ret = NvHdmiPkt_PacketWrite(pDevEvo->hdmiLib.handle,
                                 pDispEvo->displayOwner,
                                 pHeadState->activeRmId,
                                 head,
                                 hdmiLibType,
                                 hdmiLibTransmitCtrl,
-                                infoframeSize,
+                                infoframeSize +
+                                    (needChecksum ? sizeof(checksum) : 0),
                                 infoframe);
 
     if (ret != NVHDMIPKT_SUCCESS) {
-- 
